-Silo does not install agent harnesses, computer-use plugins, or MCP servers.
-Users configure compatible Linux tools themselves. Tools running in a remote
+Agent tools remain unconfigured by default. An integrator can configure the
+optional `guest/agent-tools-release.json` with a trusted full Luda source archive
+URL, SHA-256 and commit. No published artifact is assumed. When enabled, desktop
+configuration invokes the existing Luda guest bootstrap during the temporary
+VM boot; ordinary explicit desktop start/restart dispatches the same wrapper in
+the background without waiting for installation. Its lock prevents duplicate
+attempts, and dispatch is not reported as installation success. A stopped desktop
+remains pending; there is no implicit start from a tools status check.
+
+Tool state is separate from desktop/viewer state. Last-check timestamps are
+historical observations, not current readiness or host agent registration.
+Failed/interrupted attempts require inspection and explicit guest administrative
+`python3 /usr/local/lib/silo-agent-tools/agent-tools.py retry-after-review`; there
+is no automatic retry or upgrade. A changed source manifest reports an available
+update. Bootstrap configuration bundles are retained under the private
+`/var/lib/silo-agent-tools/attempt-*/bundle/config` paths. Installing the guest
+server does not register it in a host Codex profile or install a skill into the
+user's project. Users must complete placement in their intended agent context.
+The viewer remains usable when optional tools are missing or need attention. Tools running in a remote
 SSH project must execute inside the guest and target this display; selecting
 an SSH project does not redirect a macOS-only plugin. Human and automated
 input share the ordinary Linux session without Silo arbitrating control.
